      "claims": [
        {
          "id": "r0:c4430189966:0",
          "roundIndex": 0,
          "reviewId": 5720852473,
          "source": "inline",
          "commentId": 4430189966,
          "path": "src/ledger/retarget.ts",
          "line": 18,
          "severity": "P3",
          "title": "Findings on files the PR deletes are never retired",
          "text": "🟢 **P3**\n\n**Findings on files the PR deletes are never retired**\n\n`present` is built from every `filename` in the PR file list. GitHub lists a file the PR deletes with `status: \"removed\"` under its old name. `PathFile` carries no status, so a carried finding on a deleted file counts as present and stays in `kept`. It keeps blocking at `REQUEST_CHANGES`, even though a removed file cannot anchor to the head any more. That is the same stuck-at-CHANGES_REQUESTED situation as #92, reached by deleting the file instead of renaming it. Consider adding `status?: string` to `PathFile` and leaving removed files out of `present`, with a test for it."
        },
        {
          "id": "r0:c4430189972:0",
          "roundIndex": 0,
          "reviewId": 5720852473,
          "source": "inline",
          "commentId": 4430189972,
          "path": "src/review.ts",
          "line": 98,
          "severity": "P3",
          "title": "`ReviewContext.kv` is now dead state",
          "text": "🟢 **P3**\n\n**`ReviewContext.kv` is now dead state**\n\n`buildReview` no longer reads `context.kv`. The doc comment says so, yet the field stays on `ReviewContext` and `github-app.ts` still passes `kv: stateKv`. A required-looking input that does nothing invites a later reader to wire KV state back in. Remove the field and the call-site argument, since feedback writes use the separately passed client."
        },
        {
          "id": "r0:c4430189972:1",
          "roundIndex": 0,
          "reviewId": 5720852473,
          "source": "inline",
          "commentId": 4430189972,
          "path": "src/review.ts",
          "line": 98,
          "severity": "P3",
          "title": "The doc comment now says buildReview never touches `kv`, and the diff shows no remaining use in it. `github-app.ts` still passes `kv: stateKv`. This is dead state that implies a dependency that no longer exists. Remove the field and the call-site argument, or mark it reserved.",
          "text": "The doc comment now says buildReview never touches `kv`, and the diff shows no remaining use in it. `github-app.ts` still passes `kv: stateKv`. This is dead state that implies a dependency that no longer exists. Remove the field and the call-site argument, or mark it reserved."
        },
        {
          "id": "r0:c4430189972:2",
          "roundIndex": 0,
          "reviewId": 5720852473,
          "source": "inline",
          "commentId": 4430189972,
          "path": "src/review.ts",
          "line": 98,
          "severity": "P3",
          "title": "The new doc comment says `buildReview` never reads or writes `kv`. `github-app.ts` still passes `kv: stateKv` into the context anyway. The field is dead state: nothing in `buildReview` uses it, since feedback writes happen in `github-app.ts` through a separate client. A maintainer reading the type will assume it matters. Either drop it from `ReviewContext` and the call site, or mark it explicitly as reserved.",
          "text": "The new doc comment says `buildReview` never reads or writes `kv`. `github-app.ts` still passes `kv: stateKv` into the context anyway. The field is dead state: nothing in `buildReview` uses it, since feedback writes happen in `github-app.ts` through a separate client. A maintainer reading the type will assume it matters. Either drop it from `ReviewContext` and the call site, or mark it explicitly as reserved."
        },
